1.2.1.6 Arrowheads and Filled Areas
...................................

‘lines’, ‘explicit’ and ‘parametric’ accept ‘head_end=true’,
‘head_start=true’ and ‘head_count=n’ (n heads evenly spaced along the
curve by length).  Heads follow the curve's direction on screen, so they
stay correct under ‘aspect_ratio’; ‘head_size’ scales them.

By default each head follows the curve's local direction
(‘head_direction="tangent"’), as in most plotting libraries.  For wavy
or noisy curves, ‘head_direction="chord"’ points each head along the
curve's overall direction instead: from the point one head-spacing of
length back (the start of the curve, for a single ‘head_end’).

A head's tip sits on its data point and the head extends back over the
curve (Plotly can only offset markers backwards).  For a wavy line such
as a photon, end the data with a short straight run about one head long,
so the wiggle stops where the head begins, as TikZ users do with ‘post
length’:

photon: append(
  makelist([s, 0.03*sin(12*%pi*s)], s, makelist(i/100, i, 0, 100)),
  [[1.15, 0]])$
ax_draw2d(lines(photon, head_end=true, head_size=1.2), aspect_ratio=true)$

‘explicit’ accepts the draw package's ‘filled_func’: ‘filled_func=true’
fills between the curve and y = 0, and ‘filled_func=g(x)’ fills between
the curve and g, using ‘fill_color’.

ax_draw2d(
  explicit(sin(x), x, 0, %pi, filled_func=sin(x)/2, fill_color="rgba(0,0,255,0.2)"),
  parametric(cos(t), sin(t), t, 0, 3*%pi/2, head_end=true, head_count=2),
  aspect_ratio=true
)$

Like the draw package, ‘filled_func’ is sticky when given at the top
level: it applies to every later ‘explicit’ until reset with
‘filled_func=false’.


File: ax-plots.info,  Node: Shapes,  Next: Raw Plotly,  Prev: Arrowheads and Filled Areas,  Up: Examples

1.2.1.7 Shapes
..............

‘rectangle’, ‘ellipse’, ‘hline’ and ‘vline’ are drawn as Plotly layout
shapes: they never appear in the legend or take hover.  ‘rectangle’ and
‘ellipse’ take the draw package's arguments.  The outline uses ‘color’,
‘line_width’ and ‘dash’; ‘rectangle’ and ‘ellipse’ are filled only when
‘fill_color’ is set.  ‘layer="below"’ draws a shape underneath the data.
Like ‘label’, shapes are not yet supported inside ‘animate(...)’.

ax_draw2d(
  rectangle([0, 0], [1, 1], fill_color="rgba(0,150,0,0.15)", layer="below"),
  ellipse(0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.25, color="blue"),
  ellipse(0, 0, 0.3, 0.3, 0, 90, fill_color="orange"),
  hline(0.5, dash="dot"), vline(0.5, dash="dot"),
  explicit(x^2, x, 0, 1),
  aspect_ratio=true
)$


File: ax-plots.info,  Node: Raw Plotly,  Next: Data Values,  Prev: Shapes,  Up: Examples

1.2.1.8 Raw Plotly
..................

Anything not wrapped by ax-plots can be passed through as JSON:
‘ax_trace("{...}")’ adds a Plotly trace verbatim, and
‘layout_raw="{...}"’ appends keys to the layout, overriding ax-plots'
own values (annotations from ‘label’ / ‘vector’ still take precedence).

ax_draw2d(
  explicit(x^2, x, -1, 1),
  ax_trace("{\"type\":\"bar\",\"x\":[-0.5,0.5],\"y\":[0.25,0.25],\"opacity\":0.4}"),
  layout_raw="{\"hovermode\":\"x unified\"}"
)$


File: ax-plots.info,  Node: Data Values,  Next: Per-object Options,  Prev: Raw Plotly,  Up: Examples

1.2.1.9 Data Values
...................

‘lines’ and ‘points’ data may contain exact numeric constants such as
‘%pi/2’, ‘sqrt(2)’ or ‘atan(3)’; they are converted with ‘float’.  A
value that is still not a number (for example a free symbol ‘a’) is an
error naming the object, axis and index.  Use ‘false’ (or ‘inf’, ‘minf’,
‘und’, ‘ind’) for an intentional gap in a line.  Numeric constants are
also accepted in ‘xrange’ / ‘yrange’, e.g. ‘xrange=[-%pi, %pi]’.


File: ax-plots.info,  Node: Per-object Options,  Next: Programmatic Use,  Prev: Data Values,  Up: Examples

1.2.1.10 Per-object Options
...........................

Style options can be given as trailing arguments inside an object.  They
apply to that object only and do not change the options in effect for
later objects:

ax_draw2d(
  color="blue",
  lines([0, 1, 2], [0, 1, 0], color="red", dash="dot", showlegend=false),
  lines([0, 1, 2], [1, 0, 1])          /* still blue */
)$

‘showlegend’ inside an object hides (or shows) that trace's legend
entry; at the top level ‘showlegend’ is the layout option for the whole
legend.


File: ax-plots.info,  Node: Programmatic Use,  Next: Style Options 2,  Prev: Per-object Options,  Up: Examples

1.2.1.11 Programmatic Use
.........................

To build a figure in a loop, collect objects and options in a list and
pass it with ‘apply’:

objs: makelist(lines([0, 1], [0, k], color="gray", hoverinfo="skip"), k, 1, 5)$
apply(ax_draw2d, append(objs, [title="Fan", aspect_ratio=true]))$


